syntax:
To declare a function with default arguments:
return_type function_name(param1_type param1, param2_type param2 = default_value);
Practical usage notes: Default arguments must be specified in the function declaration (e.g., in a header file) and can only be at the end of the parameter list. For example: void processData(int value, std::string format = "default"); Here, 'format' has a default value, allowing callers to omit it for standard behavior. This promotes flexibility while adhering to C++'s rules for avoiding ambiguity.

code_example:
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

void greet(const std::string& name, int times = 1) {
    for(int i = 0; i < times; ++i) {
        std::cout << "Hello, " << name << std::endl;
    }
}

int main() {
    greet("Alice");       // Uses default value for times (1)
    greet("Bob", 3);     // Overrides default with 3
    return 0;
}
